+# Security audit and deployment
+
+## Scope and findings
+
+This audit covers the dependency-free Node HTTP server and the static Daily Grind page. No authentication, sessions, database, user input rendering, or third-party JavaScript exists in this repository. The account, order, and other navigation routes render the same landing page; `/logout` only returns text. They must not be treated as implemented account or commerce features.
+
+| Finding | Preventative measure |
+| --- | --- |
+| No content policy or safe rollout mechanism | CSP starts in report-only mode; explicit enforcement option, local reporting, and rollback instructions |
+| Inline stylesheet and style attribute obstruct strict CSP | Extracted stylesheet served at `/styles.css`; removed inline attribute |
+| Missing clickjacking, MIME, referrer, and feature controls | DENY framing, nosniff, no-referrer, and restricted Permissions-Policy on application responses |
+| URL parsing trusts Host and can throw | Fixed parsing origin, malformed-target rejection, no Host reflection |
+| Every path and method returns a successful landing page | Explicit page/asset allowlist, 404 responses, GET/HEAD only except report ingestion |
+| Unlimited policy report intake could expose private data or flood logs | 16 KiB bodies, 60 submissions per minute per process, validated JSON, sanitized directive/disposition only |
+| Default connection limits unsuitable for deliberate deployment | Explicit header size, header/request deadlines, keepalive and requests-per-socket limits |
+
+The CSP permits only local CSS and images from the existing `images.unsplash.com` host. Scripts, plugins, base elements, and embedding are prohibited by the candidate policy; forms are restricted to the same origin. No wildcard, unsafe-inline, or unsafe-eval allowances were added. In report-only mode CSP restrictions produce reports rather than blocking resources; X-Frame-Options independently blocks framing.
+
+## Deploy and roll back
+
+1. Use a supported Node release (22 or later), run `npm test` and `npm run build`, and deploy behind an HTTPS reverse proxy. The process itself serves HTTP; certificate management, HTTP-to-HTTPS redirects, connection limits, and edge rate limiting belong at the proxy. Preserve the application's security headers on the public response and supply equivalent headers on proxy-generated errors.
+2. Start with `CSP_MODE=report-only npm start` (also the default). Route stderr into your existing operational log collector and monitor structured `csp-violation` events, report-endpoint errors, and 429 counts. The legacy browser-compatible `report-uri /csp-report` endpoint accepts `application/csp-report` JSON. It intentionally does not accept unrelated telemetry. Verify headers with `curl -I https://YOUR_ORIGIN/` and `curl -I https://YOUR_ORIGIN/styles.css`.
+3. Observe representative traffic across releases and all deployed paths. Investigate violations before promotion; do not automatically add reported origins to the policy. Reports are attacker-controlled, may come from browser extensions, and can be missing or suppressed. An absence of reports alone does not establish compatibility. Automated HTTP tests validate routes and resource declarations; they do not execute browser CSP enforcement.
+4. Promote a staging deployment and then a limited production instance with `CSP_MODE=enforce npm start`. Check report counts and application health before wider promotion. Changing the environment requires restarting the process. Unknown mode values fail startup instead of silently weakening the policy.
+5. If a CSP regression occurs, restore `CSP_MODE=report-only` and restart. Keep baseline response headers and continue collecting reports while correcting the policy or assets. Responses use `Cache-Control: no-store` so browsers do not retain obsolete page or stylesheet policy assumptions.
+6. After HTTPS is stable for the public origin, optionally set `ENABLE_HSTS=true`. This sends a conservative one-day HSTS policy. It deliberately omits `includeSubDomains` and preload. Do not enable it on an origin that needs HTTP access. Disable the setting to stop refreshing HSTS; already stored browser policies remain until expiry. Forwarded headers do not toggle this setting.
+
+## Operational boundaries
+
+- There are no installed runtime dependencies to audit; repeat a dependency audit if dependencies are introduced.
+- The report quota is global to each process. An attacker can exhaust it and hide reports. Use proxy limits and aggregate monitoring for production; these application bounds are not comprehensive denial-of-service protection. Logs exclude document/blocked URLs and script samples to avoid persisting secrets, so investigating resource-specific violations may require an approved external monitoring workflow later.
+- No cookies or credentials are set. Real account/logout/order features require authentication, authorization, POST-based state changes, CSRF defenses, and secure session cookies when implemented. The current logout response does not terminate a session.
+- The existing remote Unsplash image remains a third-party availability and privacy dependency. Referrer information is suppressed and CSP limits its origin.
+- Security headers are defense in depth. New scripts, assets, forms, or external services require policy review and the same report-only promotion process.
+
+References: [MDN CSP guide](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Guides/CSP), [report-only header](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only), [Node HTTP server limits](https://nodejs.org/api/http.html).
